    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on a low-fat mayo formulation and having issues with phase separation in high-acid environments. Can you explain the specific molecular mechanics of how lecithin acts as an emulsifier in this context, especially regarding the oil-water interface?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin, a phospholipid, works by positioning its hydrophilic phosphate head group in the water phase and its lipophilic fatty acid tails into the oil phase at the interface. This amphiphilic nature lowers the interfacial tension between the oil and the aqueous phase of your mayo. In high-acidic conditions, lecithin's ability to form a stable, viscoelastic film around the dispersed oil droplets becomes crucial, preventing coalescence and phase separation.",
      "source": "target",
