    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'm also seeing some issues where the droplets seem to be actively aggregating or \"breaking\" the emulsion despite having enough lecithin. Is there a specific threshold of pH or ionic strength where the lecithin film fails to provide enough steric or electrostatic repulsion to keep them apart?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, drastic changes in $\\text{pH}$ or ionic strength can destabilize the lecithin film by affecting the charge on its phosphate head groups. At very low $\\text{pH}$, the phosphate groups can become fully protonated, reducing electrostatic repulsion. High ionic strength screens these surface charges, diminishing the repulsive forces and allowing van der Waals attractive forces to cause droplet aggregation and emulsion breakdown.",
      "source": "target",
