    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Got it. So if the electrostatic repulsion fails, the droplets just clump together. If I try to compensate by increasing the lecithin concentration even further, does that actually help stabilize the interface, or is there a point of diminishing returns where adding more lecithin just creates its own set of instabilities in the emulsion?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Increasing lecithin beyond a certain point, known as the critical micelle concentration (CMC) or effective interfacial coverage, may not improve stability and can actually introduce new issues. Excess emulsifier can lead to bridging flocculation, where molecules physically link droplets together, causing aggregation rather than repulsion. Furthermore, very high concentrations can sometimes alter the rheological properties unfavorably, making the system prone to destabilization through other mechanisms.",
      "source": "target",
